A basic pedicure costs $20 to $50 in the US, or £17 to £35 in the UK. That figure covers a foot soak, nail trimming and shaping, cuticle care, light exfoliation, and a coat of regular polish. It's the entry point at almost every salon, and it's what most people picture when they book "a pedicure" without specifying further.

Quora threads on the topic land in almost exactly this range, with one reader noting <strong>pedicure</strong> prices around "$25-$35... that's the average price for your everyday neighbourhood nail shop," and Airtasker's own pricing data puts the US median closer to $33 for a basic service. The variation within that window comes down to three things: how busy the salon is, how much product they use, and whether the price includes a short massage or stops right after polish.

What catches people off guard isn't the price itself, it's the gap between salons. Two shops a mile apart can both call their entry-level service a "basic pedicure" and land $20 apart. That's not necessarily a red flag. It usually means one salon is running on thinner margins in a cheaper space, and the other is covering higher rent or paying technicians more per service.

