This split trips up a lot of people, especially anyone reading a US-focused guide while living somewhere tipping culture works differently. A reader genuinely asking "how much should I tip the nail tech" after a $25 pedicure in the US should land on $4 to $5 cash, on top of the service price, not folded into it.

The reasoning behind the US norm comes down to how salon staff are typically compensated. Many nail technicians earn a lower base wage with the expectation that tips make up a real portion of their income, similar to servers. Skipping the tip in that context isn't neutral, it's effectively underpaying for the service. In the UK, technician pay structures more often build a living wage into the listed price itself, which is exactly why tipping there feels optional rather than expected.

